 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Transient Detection Holes:** In areas with frequent transient objects (e.g., clouds, tourists), strict transient detection (removing all Gaussians with non-zero variance) can leave holes (black spots) in the static reconstruction [S1].
-*   **Adaptive Threshold:** To mitigate holes, an adaptive threshold $\lambda$ can be used ($\text{Var}(\widetilde{\Delta \alpha}_I) < \lambda$) instead of requiring zero variance, but this requires manual tuning of $\lambda$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Training time almost doubles compared to standard 3DGS, and inference time per frame is 10 times longer [S1].
+*   **Transient Object Holes:** Parts of the scene with frequent transient objects (e.g., clouds, tourists) may be incorrectly detected as transient. Eliminating all transient Gaussians can leave holes (black spots) in the reconstruction [S1].
+*   **Mitigation:** To solve the hole issue, Gaussians can be removed based on an adaptive threshold $\lambda$ rather than considering only Gaussians with zero variance ($\text{Var}(\Delta \tilde{\alpha}_I) < \lambda$). However, this requires manual tuning of $\lambda$ [S1].
+*   **Transient Percentage:** On average, less than 20% of Gaussians capture transient objects in trained scenes [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is approximately 2x that of standard 3DGS [S1]. Inference time is 10x slower than 3DGS [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Increased memory usage due to the Hash Grid, per-image embeddings, and MLP parameters [S1].
+*   **Training Time:** Almost doubles compared to standard 3DGS [S1].
+*   **Inference Time:** 10 times longer per frame than 3DGS [S1].
+*   **Comparison to Baselines:** Despite the increase in time, SWAG requires significantly less training time than previous in-the-wild baselines and achieves real-time rendering [S1].
 *   **Strengths:**
-    *   Handles unstructured, in-the-wild image collections with photometric variations [S1].
-    *   Disentangles static and transient objects, allowing for occluder removal [S1].
-    *   Enables controllable appearance via interpolation of image embeddings [S1].
-    *   Faster training than previous implicit in-the-wild baselines [S1].
+    *   Handles unstructured in-the-wild data with photometric variations [S1].
+    *   Disentangles static and dynamic Gaussians, preventing blurry elongated Gaussians caused by view-dependent appearances in standard 3DGS [S1].
+    *   Enables controllable appearance via interpolation of learned image embeddings [S1].
 *   **Limitations:**
-    *   Slower inference than standard 3DGS [S1].
-    *   Requires manual tuning of the transient detection threshold $\lambda$ to avoid holes [S1].
-    *   Less than 20% of Gaussians typically capture transient objects, meaning the majority of the scene is static [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Use SWAG when reconstructing scenes from unstructured, internet-sourced photo collections (e.g., Phototourism, NeRF-OSR) where lighting varies and transient objects are present [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Avoid if real-time inference speed is critical and the scene is static and well-lit, as standard 3DGS is faster and simpler [S1].
+    *   Requires manual tuning of threshold $\lambda$ for transient object removal to avoid holes [S1].
+    *   Higher computational cost for training and inference compared to vanilla 3DGS [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When reconstructing scenes from unstructured, in-the-wild photo collections (e.g., tourist landmarks) where lighting varies and transient occluders (people, clouds) are present [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For controlled studio environments or static scenes without transient objects, where standard 3DGS is more efficient [S1].
 
 ## Background
-SWAG was introduced by H. Dahmani et al. in 2024 [S1]. It builds upon 3D Gaussian Splatting to address its poor performance on unstructured in-the-wild data [S1]. The method was evaluated on the Phototourism Dataset (Brandenburg Gate, Sacré Cœur, Trevi Fountain) and the NeRF-OSR Dataset [S1]. It represents a step towards conditioning 3DGS for in-the-wild scene representations, suggesting future research directions such as extension to dynamic scenes [S1].
+SWAG was introduced by H. Dahmani et al. in the paper "SWAG: Splatting in the Wild images with Appearance-conditioned Gaussians" [S1]. It addresses the poor performance of 3DGS on unstructured in-the-wild data by incorporating appearance modeling and unsupervised transient object handling [S1]. The method was evaluated on the Phototourism Dataset (Brandenburg Gate, Sacré Cœur, Trevi Fountain) and the NeRF-OSR Dataset [S1].
 
 ## Sources
